{# _busy_loop_calibration.j2 — size the busy-loop probe's iteration count.

   Shared verbatim by main.cc.j2 and main_aot.cc.j2 (both render it at 8-space
   indentation, immediately before the clean/power window is armed) so the two
   cannot drift apart -- the class of bug that produced the original AP4 miss.

   Timed by STIMER, never DWT.  main.cc.j2's prelude pins
   window_timer == "stimer" for this probe on every family (read it for the
   per-family reasoning and the resolution trade that choice accepts), which is
   what puts hpx_stimer_* in scope here.  The case this block exists to survive
   is the dedicated power binary, where the CoreSight debug domain is already
   gone long before this point regardless of any HAL call: AP4's broad
   peripheral shutdown disables AM_HAL_PWRCTRL_PERIPH_DEBUG at boot (~200
   rendered lines above here), and on any Cortex-M4F part a free-running power
   binary has no debugger asserting CDBGPWRUPREQ to hold it up in the first
   place.  See SocCapabilities.power_window_timer.

   Reading DWT->CYCCNT here instead (as this block did until issue #112) read a
   frozen counter: the tick delta came out 0, the scaling branch was skipped,
   the iteration count silently kept its seed value, and the window ran for a
   length unrelated to window_target_ms. #}
        // Size the busy-loop iteration count so the window fills roughly the
        // {{ window_target_ms }} ms target, then run a plain bounded counter loop
        // with no clock reads at all inside the window itself.  A live
        // "while (elapsed < target) nop();" exit condition is not an option:
        // when it read DWT it hung forever once the debug domain went off
        // (found 2026-07-03: real firmware hang, required a J-Link reset to
        // recover the board), and polling STIMER every iteration would put
        // peripheral traffic inside the measured region.
        //
        // The calibration loop must keep the SAME shape as the measured loop
        // below -- volatile counter, one nop -- or its per-iteration cost does
        // not transfer.  STIMER is the clock (see the file header): unlike
        // SystemCoreClock it is measured rather than assumed.
        //
        // The window-begin block below calls hpx_stimer_init() again -- each
        // call drops and re-requests the XT, but since #110 landed the settle
        // lives inside hpx_stimer_init() itself, so every call verifies the
        // crystal before its caller reads a tick.  Cost per call: two 10 ms
        // probes warm, up to the 1 s deadline cold -- so this probe pays it
        // twice per run (calibration + window begin), all outside measured
        // regions.
        hpx_stimer_init();
        const uint32_t busy_calib_t0 = hpx_stimer_ticks();
        volatile uint32_t busy_calib_i = 0;
        for (; busy_calib_i < 100000U; busy_calib_i++) {
            __asm volatile("nop");
        }
        const uint32_t busy_calib_ticks =
            (uint32_t)(hpx_stimer_ticks() - busy_calib_t0);
        const uint64_t clean_probe_target_ticks =
            ((uint64_t)HPX_STIMER_HZ * (uint64_t){{ window_target_ms }}U) / 1000ULL;
        // Plausibility band for the measurement above.  100000 volatile-counter
        // nop iterations cost roughly 4-8 core cycles each, so from a 24 MHz to
        // a 250 MHz core the pass lands somewhere between ~1.6 ms and ~34 ms --
        // 52 to ~1100 STIMER ticks.  A reading outside [16, 8192] ticks
        // (~0.5 ms to ~250 ms) does not describe a slow or a fast part, it
        // describes a bad measurement.
        //
        // busy_calib_ticks is a DENOMINATOR, so a bad one mis-sizes the window
        // by that same multiplicative factor -- which is exactly the failure
        // issue #112 was about, just arriving through a different door.  The
        // The remaining door for a bad reading is narrow: the settle inside
        // hpx_stimer_init() (#110, landed) verifies the crystal against the
        // BOOTROM clock before this calibration reads a tick, so the band
        // below is a second line of defence, not the first -- it still
        // refuses an implausible reading rather than scale from it.
        // rather than scale from it.  Falling back to the seed yields a short
        // (~6 ms) window, but the window is now MEASURED, so a fallback shows
        // up in HPX_CLEAN_INFER_AVG_US as the ~1000x miss it is instead of
        // hiding behind the nominal target.
        const uint32_t busy_calib_min_ticks = 16U;
        const uint32_t busy_calib_max_ticks = 8192U;
        uint32_t busy_loop_iters = 100000U;
        if (busy_calib_ticks >= busy_calib_min_ticks &&
            busy_calib_ticks <= busy_calib_max_ticks) {
            const uint64_t scaled =
                ((uint64_t)busy_calib_i * clean_probe_target_ticks) /
                (uint64_t)busy_calib_ticks;
            busy_loop_iters = (scaled > 0xFFFFFFFFULL) ? 0xFFFFFFFFU : (uint32_t)scaled;
        }
